    {
      "id": "clear-ghost-data",
      "title": "How to clear ghost data in Pokémon RSE and FR/LG",
      "author": "",
      "addedOn": "2026-07-18",
      "category": "Preparation",
      "difficulty": "Beginner",
      "summary": "\"Ghost data\" is invisible leftover data from a Pokémon that can appear after migrating Pokémon to gen 4 through Pal Park, or from glitches such as the Glitzer popping in Pokémon Emerald that unlocks ACE. For Ruby, Sapphire or Emerald, you should clear invisible leftover data from last row of box 11 until box 14 last row, as that is the execution zone. In Fire Red / Leaf Green, the execution zone is from the second row of box 13, until last row of box 14. \nAce codes read the content of these boxes, so any random Pokémon or leftover data may interupt the code and cause a freeze or crash.",
      "introduction": "",
      "video": "https://youtu.be/rlokXgSXGaI",
      "body": "-Make sure boxes 11-14 are empty (box 13 and 14 for FR/LG). \n-Find a box full of Pokemon, or the fullest box you have.\n-Press \"select\" to go into multi-select option. The hand will become yellow.\n-press and hold A on a Pokémon in the corner of your box, and drag across to the opposite corner. Let go of A to pick up all selected Pokémon.\n-Drop them inside all of the slots inside the mentioned boxes.\n-Put these Pokémon back to where you had them, making sure boxes 11-14 are empty.\n\n-If you are doing this because of a code that isn't working, or is causing a freeze or crash, you should now save and try the ACE code again. If the code still isn't working, you can at least be sure it was not because of ghost data. Follow the troubleshooting page for more info on how to fix your code."
    },
    {
      "id": "guide-how-to-execute-codes-with-the-0x410e-stable-arm-species-1784293726776",
      "title": "Using the 0x410e Stable ARM species for ACE codes",
      "author": "",
      "addedOn": "2026-07-18",
      "category": "Code execution",
      "difficulty": "Beginner",
      "summary": "About the stable ARM species 0x410e and how to use it to execute ACE codes in Pokémon Emerald for the English language version.",
      "introduction": "The 0x410e is a Stable ARM species found by Mettrich. Because it is ARM and not THUMB, we do not need a THUMB - ARM bootstrap for this species, unlike the stable 0x40e9 that is a stable THUMB species. Because it is stable, it also allows us to view the summary without the game crashing. It also has access to some modern/shortened codes that the unstable Dots egg 0x611 ARM species cannot execute.",
      "video": "https://youtu.be/dRo4bpyWQXw",
      "body": "- You may execute codes with the Stable ARM 0x410e species anywhere, unless a code requires a specific location. \n- You can execute codes from your party or inside the pc; anywhere you can open the summary of the species. \n- Like most regular ACE codes, you need box 11 last row, until last row of box 14 to be empty. \n- If you are using special codes that require specific bootstraps or payloads, you will generally put these somewhere within boxes 12-14, but box 12 slot 9 and 10 must be empty no matter what.\n\nWhile viewing its summary, wait until the sprite disappears (around 1 second). If you close the summary too fast, a code may never be triggered."
    },
    {
      "id": "guide-how-to-remove-bad-eggs-in-firered-leafgreen-1786028458577",
      "title": "How to remove Bad Eggs in FireRed & LeafGreen.",
      "author": "",
      "addedOn": "2026-08-06",
      "category": "Clone/Declone",
      "difficulty": "Intermediate",
      "summary": "Removing Bad Eggs using a decloner species made from the mail glitch.",
      "introduction": "A Bad Egg is a placeholder that appears when a Pokémon’s data fails its checksum, indicating corrupted or invalid data. In FireRed and LeafGreen, Bad Eggs commonly appear when viewing the stable ACE species 0x351 while executing codes. Bad Eggs are not harmful, but they can be annoying and take up unnecessary space in the PC boxes. Sometimes, they are deliberately used as a payload, script, or bootstrap for ACE codes.",
      "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XRbUgP0Xb9o",
      "body": "There are two main ways of deleting Bad Eggs in Pokémon FireRed & LeafGreen. Try the first one, and if it doesn't work, you have to use the second method.\n\n\n1. Multi-selection grab and drop\n- While in the \"Move Pokémon\" mode inside a PC, press *Select* (Y on Nintendo Switch) to enter the multi-selection tool (hand becomes yellow). \n- Hover over the Bad Egg, press and HOLD A. The egg should now be picked up. \n- While holding A, move the glove in any direction so it hovers over a different slot.\n- Let go of A to put down the egg, and it will disappear. In some cases this will not work. If so, continue with method 2:\n\n2. Clonator/Decloner\nTo do this, you must already have set up the mail glitch in-game. This will also work pre-E4, but in some cases you need to have seen specific Pokémon to unlock words needed to type in the mail.\n\n- Make sure PC Box 3 slot 1 is empty.\n- Have six Pokémon in your team, and equip Retro Mail on all of them. When asked about a message for the first 5, just select anything and proceed. The last mail you equip should be glitched with question marks: \"???\". Depending on your game language, you will create a specific glitch species called a Clonator, or Decloner. You will enter the following words:\n\nEnglish (0x3200):\n\nWANDERING  |  ???\nMEET       |  LISTEN\n\nGerman (0x320F):\n\nANGST BIBBER | AQUAWELLE\nWÜTEND       | ZAPDOS\n\nSpanish (0x3210):\n\nMAREADO     | HIDROPULSO\nREFRESCANTE | ARTICUNO\n\nItalian (0x320A):\n\nCOCCOLONE | FOGLIAMAGICA\nADORARE   | TOGEPI\n\nFrench (0x320B):\n\nJOIE   |  VIBRAQUA\nGENIAL |  DRACOLOSSE\n\n----------------------\n\n- Confirm, and check Box 3 slot 1 for a question mark Pokémon. This is your Clonator/Decloner species. It can be used to clone or remove any Pokémon or bad egg.\n- It is recommended that you move it to slot 30 of a box you don't use often, because viewing it accidentally may clone Pokémon. \n- Exit the PC and open \"Move Pokémon\" again to undo unwanted effects from viewing the species. \n- It is recommended that you place the bad egg you want to remove in the same box and row as the decloner, for example slot 28.\n- Save before attempting to delete, in case you delete something you don't want to delete. \n- Enter the multi-select mode again while holding A, move the glove one slot to the right and let go of A to pick up the bad egg. While holding the egg, move the glove over the decloner species, and press A to put it down. It should now disappear. Watch the video example below for a visual demonstration."
    }
